      stepByStepSolution: "Correct Answer: (A) All of them have exactly 1 valence electron in their outermost shell, which they readily lose to form univalent cations (\$\\text{M}^+\$).\n\nScientific Principle / Key Concept:\n• Lithium (\$Z=3\$): \$2, 1\$\n• Sodium (\$Z=11\$): \$2, 8, 1\$\n• Potassium (\$Z=19\$): \$2, 8, 8, 1\$\nSimilarity: All three elements have 1 electron in their valence shell. Because they have the same valence electronic configuration, they lose this single electron with ease to attain a noble gas structure, reacting vigorously with water to form basic hydroxides and hydrogen gas:\n\$\$2\\text{M}(s) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\rightarrow 2\\text{MOH}(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\$\$\n\nDetailed Distractor & Misconception Analysis:\n• Option (B): Incorrect. Number of shells increases: 2, 3, 4.\n• Option (C): Incorrect. They have 1 electron in their outer shell.\n• Option (D): Incorrect. They have different atomic masses.",
