      stepByStepSolution: "Correct Answer: (A) Both have completely filled outermost valence shells (He has a filled duplet in the K shell; Ne has a filled octet in the L shell), conferring zero chemical valency.\n\nScientific Principle / Key Concept:\n• Helium (\$Z=2\$): Electronic configuration is \$2\$. Its outermost K shell can hold a maximum of 2 electrons (\$2n^2 = 2(1)^2 = 2\$). Thus, its outermost shell is completely filled (stable duplet).\n• Neon (\$Z=10\$): Electronic configuration is \$2, 8\$. Its outermost L shell is completely filled with 8 electrons (stable octet).\n• Common feature: Both atoms have completely filled outermost shells, which gives them maximum stability and zero valency, explaining their chemical unreactivity.\n\nDetailed Distractor & Misconception Analysis:\n• Option (B): Incorrect. He has 2, not 8 valence electrons.\n• Option (C): Incorrect. He has 1 shell; Ne has 2 shells.\n• Option (D): Incorrect. Noble gases are monoatomic.",
