        QuestionOption(id: "B", text: "Boron (\$\\text{B}\$) and Carbon (\$\\text{C}\$)", isCorrect: false),
        QuestionOption(id: "C", text: "Hydrogen (\$\\text{H}\$) and Helium (\$\\text{He}\$)", isCorrect: false),
        QuestionOption(id: "D", text: "Sodium (\$\\text{Na}\$) and Magnesium (\$\\text{Mg}\$)", isCorrect: false),
      ],
      stepByStepSolution: "Correct Answer: (A) Lithium (\$\\text{Li}\$, \$Z=3\$) and Beryllium (\$\\text{Be}\$, \$Z=4\$)\n\nScientific Principle / Key Concept:\nThe first ten elements are:\n1. \$\\text{H}\$ (Hydrogen) - Non-metal\n2. \$\\text{He}\$ (Helium) - Non-metal (noble gas)\n3. \$\\text{Li}\$ (Lithium) - Metal (Alkali metal)\n4. \$\\text{Be}\$ (Beryllium) - Metal (Alkaline earth metal)\n5. \$\\text{B}\$ (Boron) - Metalloid\n6. \$\\text{C}\$ (Carbon) - Non-metal\n7. \$\\text{N}\$ (Nitrogen) - Non-metal\n8. \$\\text{O}\$ (Oxygen) - Non-metal\n9. \$\\text{F}\$ (Fluorine) - Non-metal (halogen)\n10. \$\\text{Ne}\$ (Neon) - Non-metal (noble gas)\nHence, only Lithium (\$\\text{Li}\$) and Beryllium (\$\\text{Be}\$) are metals.\n\nDetailed Distractor & Misconception Analysis:\n• Option (B): Boron is a metalloid and carbon is a non-metal.\n• Option (C): H and He are non-metals.\n• Option (D): Na and Mg have \$Z=11\$ and \$12\$, exceeding 10.",
      difficultyLevel: DifficultyLevel.easy,
      status: QuestionStatus.approved,
      submittedBy: '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
      reviewedBy: '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
      createdAt: DateTime.fromMillisecondsSinceEpoch(1727200000000 + 14000),
