        QuestionOption(id: "B", text: "Selenium (\$\\text{Se}\$)", isCorrect: false),
        QuestionOption(id: "C", text: "Arsenic (\$\\text{As}\$)", isCorrect: false),
        QuestionOption(id: "D", text: "Germanium (\$\\text{Ge}\$)", isCorrect: false),
      ],
      stepByStepSolution: "Correct Answer: (A) Beryllium (\$\\text{Be}\$)\n\nScientific Principle / Key Concept:\n• Positions in Periodic Table:\n  - \$\\text{Be}\$: Group 2, Period 2\n  - \$\\text{Ga}\$: Group 13, Period 4\n  - \$\\text{Ge}\$: Group 14, Period 4\n  - \$\\text{As}\$: Group 15, Period 4\n  - \$\\text{Se}\$: Group 16, Period 4\n• Trends in Metallic Character:\n  1. Across a period (left to right): Metallic character decreases as effective nuclear charge increases.\n     In Period 4: \$\\text{Ga} > \\text{Ge} > \\text{As} > \\text{Se}\$.\n  2. Comparing \$\\text{Be}\$ and \$\\text{Ga}\$:\n     \$\\text{Be}\$ is in Group 2 (alkaline earth metal), situated further to the left than Group 13 (\$\\text{Ga}\$). Group 2 elements are electropositive metals, whereas Group 13 elements possess higher electronegativity and amphoteric character. Hence, Beryllium has the maximum metallic characteristic among the given choices.\n\nDetailed Distractor & Misconception Analysis:\n• Option (B): Se is a non-metal.\n• Option (C): As is a metalloid.\n• Option (D): Ge is a metalloid.",
      difficultyLevel: DifficultyLevel.hots,
      status: QuestionStatus.approved,
      submittedBy: '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
      reviewedBy: '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
      createdAt: DateTime.fromMillisecondsSinceEpoch(1727200000000 + 15000),
