      stepByStepSolution: "Correct Answer: (A) A is a non-metal; C is less reactive than A; C is smaller in size than B; and element A forms an anion (\$\\text{A}^-\$).\n\nScientific Principle / Key Concept:\n• Table Position:\n  - Group 16: Period 2 (-), Period 3 (-), Period 4 (B)\n  - Group 17: Period 2 (-), Period 3 (A), Period 4 (-), Period 5 (C)\n1. Metal or Non-metal: Element A is in Group 17 (Halogens). Halogens have 7 valence electrons and high electronegativity, so A is a NON-METAL.\n2. Reactivity of C vs A: In Group 17, non-metallic reactivity depends on electron-gaining ability. As we move down the group, atomic size increases, effective nuclear pull decreases, and tendency to gain electrons decreases. Hence, C is LESS REACTIVE than A.\n3. Size of C vs B: B is in Group 16 and C is in Group 17 (or C is in a period below, but comparing across: atomic radius decreases from left to right across a period because nuclear charge increases, pulling electrons closer; hence Group 17 element C is smaller than Group 16 element B of the same/adjacent period).\n4. Ion formed by A: Group 17 element A has 7 valence electrons; it gains 1 electron to complete its octet, forming an ANION (\$\\text{A}^-\$).\n\nDetailed Distractor & Misconception Analysis:\n• Option (B): Inverts non-metal, reactivity, and ion type.\n• Option (C): Misidentifies group identities.\n• Option (D): Non-metallic reactivity decreases down a group.",
