      stepByStepSolution: "Correct Answer: (A) Nitrogen: \$2, 5\$; Phosphorus: \$2, 8, 5\$. Nitrogen is more electronegative because its atomic radius is smaller, allowing the nucleus to attract shared electrons more strongly.\n\nScientific Principle / Key Concept:\n• Electronic configurations:\n  - Nitrogen (\$Z=7\$): \$2, 5\$ (2 occupied shells: K, L)\n  - Phosphorus (\$Z=15\$): \$2, 8, 5\$ (3 occupied shells: K, L, M)\n• Electronegativity is the tendency of an atom to attract shared pair of electrons toward itself.\n• Moving down Group 15 from N to P, an additional electron shell is added, which increases atomic size and nuclear shielding.\n• Because nitrogen has a smaller atomic size, its nucleus is closer to the valence shell and exerts a much stronger pull on shared electron pairs.\n• Therefore, Nitrogen is more electronegative than Phosphorus.\n\nDetailed Distractor & Misconception Analysis:\n• Option (B): Incorrect. Distance and shielding overcome extra nuclear charge down a group.\n• Option (C): Incorrect electronic configurations.\n• Option (D): Incorrect. N and P are non-metals.",
